        "original_id": "b2cc1701-008e-5fc9-a097-171959eea896",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Places: Indiana\n- People: Kickapoo people, Anishinaabeg\n- Topics: Indian removals in Indiana\n- Concepts: Algonquian-speaking tribes, Indigenous Mexican tribes, tribe names\n\nFacts:\n- Some Indian removals in Indiana occurred before 1830, but most removals in Indiana took place between 1830 and 1846.\n- The Kickapoo people are an Algonquian-speaking Native American and Indigenous Mexican tribe.\n- The Kickapoo people call themselves Kiikaapoa or Kiikaapoi.\n- Anishinaabeg say the name Kickapoo, rendered as Giiwigaabaw in Anishinaabe and cognate with the Kickapoo form Kiwikapawa, means \"Stands here and there.\"\n- Anishinaabeg say the name Kickapoo may refer to the Kickapoo people's migratory patterns.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "1dfd6dee-d0dd-5fa5-bb14-14ec853f9029",
        "text": "Page: Indian removals in Indiana. Sentence: Some of the removals occurred prior to 1830, but most took place between 1830 and 1846.\nPage: Kickapoo people. Sentence: The Kickapoo people (Kickapoo: Kiikaapoa or Kiikaapoi) are an Algonquian-speaking Native American and Indigenous Mexican tribe. \"\nPage: Kickapoo people. Sentence: Anishinaabeg\" say the name \"Kickapoo\" (\"Giiwigaabaw\" in the Anishinaabe language and its Kickapoo cognate \"Kiwikapawa\") means \"Stands here and there,\" which may have referred to the tribe's migratory patterns.",
